WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.735
-Our first guests tonight
starred in one of the most

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:04.937
iconic movies of all-time,
"Ghostbusters."

00:00:04.937 --> 00:00:07.974
Now they're back in
"Ghostbusters: Afterlife,"

00:00:07.974 --> 00:00:09.842
which is in theaters
November 19th.

00:00:09.842 --> 00:00:11.444
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.444 --> 00:00:13.613
-Guys.
-Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa!

00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:15.314
-Aah!

00:00:15.314 --> 00:00:17.517
-[ Gasps ]
It has a gunner's seat?

00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:19.886
[ Grunting ]

00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:25.558 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:26.893
[ Cap creaks ]

00:00:26.893 --> 00:00:28.727
[ Ghost screams ]

00:00:28.727 --> 00:00:31.297
-Go, go, go!
[ Engine revving ]

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:33.199
-Please welcome to the show --
I hope I'm saying

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:35.735
their names right --
Bill Murray, Dan Aykroyd

00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:36.969
and Ernie Hudson.

00:00:36.969 --> 00:00:39.305
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:49.315 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:59.325 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:07.934 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:12.705
[ Cheers and applause ]
Welcome, gentlemen!

00:01:12.705 --> 00:01:15.041
Uh, you know,
I was watching the film,

00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:16.275
I was telling
you guys backstage.

00:01:16.275 --> 00:01:18.544
It was very emotional for me
to watch the film.

00:01:18.544 --> 00:01:20.647
I can only imagine
how emotional it was

00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:24.083
for you guys to get back
together again.

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:25.684
-Just like --
It felt like yesterday.

00:01:25.684 --> 00:01:27.319
It felt like we never
left the packs off

00:01:27.319 --> 00:01:28.754
and never got out of
the jumpsuits.

00:01:28.754 --> 00:01:30.223
Didn't it feel like it was --

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:32.659
we were military
called back to service.

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:33.793
It felt like that.

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:35.294
You know, we were called
from the reserves.

00:01:35.294 --> 00:01:36.996
-Right, exactly.
[ Light laughter ]

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:39.399
-It was painful.
It was painful is what it was.

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:40.599
[ Laughter ]

00:01:40.599 --> 00:01:43.169
It was physically
and emotionally painful.

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:46.072
You forget how heavy it is
to carry a small refrigerator

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:48.073
on your back
for hours and hours a day.

00:01:48.073 --> 00:01:50.609
And then they say, "Okay, guys,
get down on the ground."

00:01:50.609 --> 00:01:51.811
-Yeah.
-"Cut!

00:01:51.811 --> 00:01:54.414
All right, stand up."
-No.

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:55.882
-"And then,
get down on the ground again."

00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:57.016
That's what we did.
And I went,

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:58.718
"[Bleep] we did this
20 years ago."

00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:00.153
-Yeah.
-We did a long time ago.

00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:01.754
It's basically you're
falling down on the ground

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:04.090
and having to get up with a
small refrigerator on your back.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:05.525
[ Laughter ]
-It seems like the magic

00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:07.460
of moviemaking is,
they could've made

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.662
a lighter refrigerator
for your back.

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:11.898
-They had rubber ones,
but they don't --

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:13.333
when they're on,
it doesn't look like

00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:14.667
you're carrying the weight
with the rubber ones.

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:15.734
-Yeah.
-So, we always --

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:17.503
because we're actors,
we're committed.

00:02:17.503 --> 00:02:18.971
[ Laughter ]
Yeah, put the heavy one on.

00:02:18.971 --> 00:02:20.740
It'll look more real.
Sure.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.575
-A little better
than the first -- the first --

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:24.443
-Yeah, the first one
weighed about

00:02:24.443 --> 00:02:26.278
40-something pounds
or something.

00:02:26.278 --> 00:02:28.414
We used to have it wear it for
like 14 hours a day.

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:30.049
-Yeah.
-So, it sounds crazy,

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:33.119
but, like, if you're a mother
and you have a baby,

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:34.553
and you carry your baby,
you know how that feels

00:02:34.553 --> 00:02:35.788
after a few months.

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:38.291
Imagine that your child's born
at 40 pounds.

00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:42.095
[ Laughter ]

00:02:42.095 --> 00:02:43.429
-I still don't think
you're gonna get

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:45.298
a lot of sympathy from mothers
on that, Bill, but --

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:47.033
[ Laughter ]
-No.

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:48.434
-They're like,
"I thought I had it bad

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:49.969
till I heard about
the Ghostbusters."

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:52.805
[ Laughter ]

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:55.007
So this was sort of
the first big-budget film

00:02:55.007 --> 00:02:56.341
that you had ever done, Ernie.

00:02:56.341 --> 00:02:58.878
Did you have any sense
of what you were getting into?

00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:01.247
-No. Well, I did "Spacehunter"
with Peter Strauss,

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:02.581
Molly Ringwald,
that Ivan Reitman produced.

00:03:02.581 --> 00:03:04.751
-Never heard of it. Never.
-Neither has anybody else.

00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:07.186
[ Laughter ]
But -- But the year before --

00:03:07.186 --> 00:03:09.955
So but this was --
Yeah, this was pretty amazing.

00:03:09.955 --> 00:03:12.725
Pretty incredible, yeah.
-You know, I was thinking back.

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:14.727
Like, when I think about
growing up in the suburbs

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:16.528
and wanting to live
in New York City, it's never,

00:03:16.528 --> 00:03:19.766
like, the way Woody Allen
made it look beautiful.

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:24.103
It was jokes by David Letterman
about how garbage a city it was

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:27.640
and then "Ghostbusters," where
it was a city full of ghosts.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.008
[ Laughter ]
And yet when I watched it,

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:30.443
I was like,
"That's where I wanted to live."

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:32.911
I mean, obviously, New York
was a huge part of this film,

00:03:32.911 --> 00:03:35.848
of the first one.
Was it exciting for you guys --

00:03:35.848 --> 00:03:37.617
I guess you'd been gone
from "SNL" about five years,

00:03:37.617 --> 00:03:39.451
to come back to the city
and make it.

00:03:39.451 --> 00:03:42.989
-Oh, sure. Well, any time
in New York City is exciting.

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:46.492
Uh, I love --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:48.660
Love New Yorkers.
Love the architecture.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.062
Not sure
that I would ever really

00:03:50.062 --> 00:03:51.697
live here again full-time.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:51.697 --> 00:03:54.733
But we had a great time.
It was wonderful because

00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:57.103
we would be shooting
in civilian clothes,

00:03:57.103 --> 00:03:58.604
and nobody would be
really looking,

00:03:58.604 --> 00:03:59.939
and then we'd get on the packs
and the uniforms,

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:03.242
running down the street,
and people would kind of go,

00:04:03.242 --> 00:04:04.910
"Are they exterminators?
Are they -- Well..."

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:06.945
[ Laughter ]
And then the Cadillac,

00:04:06.945 --> 00:04:08.514
we'd be going all over town
in the Cadillac.

00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:09.982
And there was hardly --

00:04:09.982 --> 00:04:12.518
"That's just another
emergency vehicle, I guess."

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:14.988
You know, New Yorkers
were so blasé about it.

00:04:14.988 --> 00:04:17.990
They weren't really impressed
at all with us at the time.

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:20.293
-It was a grand time
to be in New York.

00:04:20.293 --> 00:04:23.095
We lived in New York
for a long, long time.

00:04:23.095 --> 00:04:25.631
And to be able to be
a true New Yorker and to drive

00:04:25.631 --> 00:04:27.967
and just completely
ignore stoplights

00:04:27.967 --> 00:04:29.602
everywhere you went.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:31.537
And in that vehicle, nobody --

00:04:31.537 --> 00:04:33.872
We all looked so legit
in the damn thing, you know?

00:04:33.872 --> 00:04:35.575
And we had the refrigerator
on our back,

00:04:35.575 --> 00:04:37.743
so people would just let you go.

00:04:37.743 --> 00:04:39.912
Police would kind of go,
you know, "What the hell?"

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:41.681
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:04:41.681 --> 00:04:44.317
-But I was surprised, 'cause,
you know, I think the movie,

00:04:44.317 --> 00:04:47.286
you watch it now, and you assume
that you were fully permitted

00:04:47.286 --> 00:04:48.988
and you had a key to the city.

00:04:48.988 --> 00:04:51.324
But you were kind of
fly-by-night.

00:04:51.324 --> 00:04:52.992
-Stealing things.
-We had the key to the city,

00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:55.160
but they didn't know.
-Okay.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:57.030
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:00.366
-Right, they did not give you --
you took the key to the city.

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:03.402
-It was auxiliary kind of thing,
volunteer kind of stuff.

00:05:03.402 --> 00:05:05.638
You know?
-You shot in 30 Rock.

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:07.640
And, you know, obviously,
you guys had worked here,

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:10.242
Dan and Bill.
But did you have --

00:05:10.242 --> 00:05:12.511
-We did not have a permit for
the stuff we shot around here.

00:05:12.511 --> 00:05:13.679
-Really?
-Yeah, we just jumped out,

00:05:13.679 --> 00:05:15.347
and we were like,
"Hi! It's Dan and Bill.

00:05:15.347 --> 00:05:17.549
We're shooting.
You know, nobody's gonna --"

00:05:17.549 --> 00:05:20.385
You know, just kept going.
-Yeah, you can see the scenes

00:05:20.385 --> 00:05:22.588
on Fifth Avenue when
we're figuring out the mortgage

00:05:22.588 --> 00:05:24.990
and how to buy the equipment
and all that.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:27.359
And we're just walking there.
And people -- it's real people.

00:05:27.359 --> 00:05:30.729
We're walking there,
and they were totally oblivious

00:05:30.729 --> 00:05:32.398
to the fact it was a film unit.

00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:34.032
So what you're seeing
is real people

00:05:34.032 --> 00:05:35.367
just on the street in New York.

00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:37.036
And we were stealing everything
all over the place.

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:39.404
[ Laughter ]
Yep.

00:05:39.404 --> 00:05:41.707
-Much like in the film,
you were sort of the last

00:05:41.707 --> 00:05:43.842
Ghostbuster to join the team,
and you were coming into

00:05:43.842 --> 00:05:47.079
a situation where the other guys
knew each other.

00:05:47.079 --> 00:05:49.214
Did you feel welcome
when you came on the set, Ernie?

00:05:49.214 --> 00:05:54.186
-Yeah, I felt like the neighbor
who goes to a friend's house,

00:05:54.186 --> 00:05:55.454
and they're grabbing
all the food,

00:05:55.454 --> 00:05:57.089
and you're trying to be polite
at the table.

00:05:57.089 --> 00:05:58.891
[ Laughter ]
You know what I'm saying?

00:05:58.891 --> 00:06:01.060
So, yeah, I was welcome,

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:04.496
but obviously, you know,
I got through it.

00:06:04.496 --> 00:06:07.533
Harold would always say,
"Ernie, it's okay."

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:09.168
[ Laughter ]

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:12.671
-You reference Harold Ramis,
who sadly passed away.

00:06:12.671 --> 00:06:16.108
He was the other Ghostbuster.
I imagine it's impossible

00:06:16.108 --> 00:06:18.877
to go back to a film like this,
that you wrote with him,

00:06:18.877 --> 00:06:21.680
Dan, and not think of him.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:23.749
-Harold was a huge part,
of course,

00:06:23.749 --> 00:06:26.051
of the first "Ghostbusters"
and the second one,

00:06:26.051 --> 00:06:28.487
because of his -- just his
incredible intelligence,

00:06:28.487 --> 00:06:31.457
his great sense of humor,
his frame of reference.

00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:34.493
He -- You know, he was
a terrific co-writer,

00:06:34.493 --> 00:06:36.996
and really a lead writer,
and once I handed him

00:06:36.996 --> 00:06:38.897
that big, thick script,
he just knew,

00:06:38.897 --> 00:06:40.099
"Yeah, we can do
this, this, and this,

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:42.434
make this work this way."
We sat down with Ivan,

00:06:42.434 --> 00:06:44.102
wrote it in the basement
in Martha's Vineyard

00:06:44.102 --> 00:06:47.640
for about three months --
or, a month it was, actually.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:48.808
Three weeks.
-That's enough.

00:06:48.808 --> 00:06:52.444
He's passed away.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:52.444 --> 00:06:56.482
-And, yeah, he's the man, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:56.482 --> 00:06:58.717
-It is a little overkill
at this point, yeah.

00:06:58.717 --> 00:07:00.152
-But he's still with us,
as you'll see.

00:07:00.152 --> 00:07:01.354
-You could have said that
a long time ago.

00:07:01.354 --> 00:07:02.555
He would've liked that.
-I did!

00:07:02.555 --> 00:07:03.822
-He would've loved that.
He'd have loved

00:07:03.822 --> 00:07:06.392
to have heard that.
-Bill, did you know

00:07:06.392 --> 00:07:07.926
they were working
on a "Ghostbusters" film

00:07:07.926 --> 00:07:09.362
and had plans for you
to be in it?

00:07:09.362 --> 00:07:12.064
Is that something where like,
"Hey, you know, Harold and I

00:07:12.064 --> 00:07:14.033
are writing a film called
'Ghostbusters'"?

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:16.102
-Well, I think I knew
before Harold knew,

00:07:16.102 --> 00:07:18.237
because Danny sent me something.
And I think --

00:07:18.237 --> 00:07:20.072
There's an allegation
that John Belushi

00:07:20.072 --> 00:07:22.842
was gonna be invited, but --
Another guy whose passed away,

00:07:22.842 --> 00:07:24.277
and we don't owe him anything.
-Yeah.

00:07:24.277 --> 00:07:25.778
[ Laughter ]

00:07:25.778 --> 00:07:30.115
-But the original script
had about 27 --

00:07:30.115 --> 00:07:32.851
The original thing Danny sent me
had about 27 pages in it.

00:07:32.851 --> 00:07:35.121
And it was the funniest thing
I'd ever read in my life,

00:07:35.121 --> 00:07:36.656
and I said, "Let's go."

00:07:36.656 --> 00:07:38.257
And then the stories
get better from there.

00:07:38.257 --> 00:07:39.358
[ Laughter ]

00:07:39.358 --> 00:07:41.360
-I have more
to ask these legends.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:43.062
We'll be right back after this.
[ Cheers and applause ]

